                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                and every such
                = succeed ")." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p
            See
            %a{:href => "#TG"} Tarski-Givant
            for a thorough discussion of this and related axiom systems, and an extensive bibliography.
          %p
            One important aspect of the meta-theory lies in the relation of models of this theory to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+closed+fields"} real closed fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a real closed field, then there is a corresponding model of EPG whose underlying set is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ×
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \times R
            and where the relations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are interpreted by expected conditions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi w
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi iff
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi w
                    %mn 2
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(x, y, z, w) \;\;\; iff \;\;\; (x_1 - y_1)^2 + (x_2 - y_2)^2 = (z_1 - w_1)^2 + (z_2 - w_2)^2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi iff
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 1
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∧
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B(x, y, z) \;\;\; iff \;\;\; (x_1 - y_1)(y_2 - z_2) = (x_2 - y_2)(y_1 - z_1) \; \wedge \; 0 \leq (x_1 - y_1)(y_1 - z_1) \; \wedge \; 0 \leq (x_2 - y_2)(y_2 - z_2).
          %p
            It is mostly straightforward to verify that this structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo ×
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \times R
            satisfies the EPG axioms (all but the cut schema hold for any ordered field). More importantly,
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For every model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              of EPG there is a real closed field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi R
                    %mo ×
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \cong R \times R
              as models.
          %p
            Of course there is some arbitrariness involved in the choice of isomorphism; for example, any line segment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y
            can be used to stipulate a unit length and a coordinate axis. The proof is lightly sketched in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Tar"} Tarski
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p The significance of this result is that EPG is a complete and decidable theory, just as the theory of real closed fields is complete and decidable. Hence there exists an algorithm which, on being fed a sentence in the language of Elementary Plane Geometry, will produce a proof of the sentence or a proof of its negation in the theory.
          %h3#MacLaneAxioms Mac Lane’s axioms
          %p
            To be written, see
            %a{:href => "#MacLane"} Mac Lane (1959)
            for details.
